Evangeline
==========

Evangeline 1.1.5 (reborn) is a modern IRCNet-oriented C++ botnet project. It is
a private research and operations fork of Psotnic that keeps the original
partyline, encrypted botnet, channel protection, userlist replication, and
module model, while rebuilding the code for current networks.

The name follows the same idea that Evangeline once brought to Eggdrop years
ago: take a proven IRC bot core, preserve what made it useful, and reshape it
for the networks and operational style people actually use now.

This fork credits the original Psotnic author Grzegorz Rusin and contributors,
then adds the Evangeline work by Me.

See `EVANGELINE.md` for project background and direction.

What changed
------------

- Rebranded runtime, partyline, build banner, and documentation to Evangeline
  1.1.5 (reborn).
- SSL is enabled by default for botnet and partyline links.
- `listen` is now the SSL listener; separate `ssl_listen` configuration is no
  longer needed for normal deployments.
- IPv6 is first-class: `myipv6` makes listeners and IRC server selection
  IPv6-only, skips IPv4 fallback, and supports IPv6 bot links.
- Hostname server entries resolve according to the configured address family.
- Botnet `hub` links use SSL by default without requiring an `ssl:` prefix.
- Added automatic bot host registration for linked slave/leaf bots.
- Added automatic self-host registration when a bot connects to IRC, so it can
  join configured channels without manual owner intervention.
- Added `.link-slave` and `.link-leaf` helpers to create bot handles,
  passwords, flags, and pending IRC host auto-add state.
- Added fast leaf fallback: leaf bots try their primary slave, then other known
  slaves, then the hub as last resort, and return to the primary when it is
  back.
- Added `-n` fast hub bootstrap for first launch: ownerpass, owner handle, user
  password, config encryption, user creation, and startup in one flow.
- Improved IRCNet-oriented OP request and defensive timing while respecting IRC
  mode limits such as three `+o` targets per MODE.
- Improved build/configure output, default OpenSSL detection, and distro package
  hints for Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora, and Arch.
- Kept `make` and `make debug` as the main build targets.

Build
-----

Run `./configure` once, then:

```
make
make debug
make install PREFIX=/opt/evangeline
```

Useful configure flags:

```
./configure
./configure --with-ipv6
./configure --disable-adns
./configure --with-firedns
```

SSL is enabled by default. Use `./configure --without-ssl` only for a non-SSL
legacy build.

Package hints:

```
Debian/Ubuntu: build-essential libssl-dev
Fedora:        gcc-c++ make openssl-devel
Arch:          base-devel openssl
```

First hub bootstrap
-------------------

For a fresh hub, use `-n`:

```
./bin/evangeline -n conf.hub
```

It asks for:

```
Owner password
Owner handle
Owner user password
```

If the config is plaintext, Evangeline encrypts it, starts the hub, creates the
owner, gives the owner partyline privileges, and leaves the hub ready for SSL
partyline and bot links.

Partyline
---------

Connect with an SSL-capable client to the hub listener:

```
openssl s_client -quiet -connect '[IPv6-or-host]:43917'
```

Normal owner login sequence:

```
PASS <ownerpass>
NICK <handle>
PASS <user-password>
```

Common commands:

```
.help
.status
.whois <handle>
.mjoin #channel
.channels
.channels #channel
.link-slave <bot-handle> <link-password>
.link-leaf <bot-handle> <link-password>
.save
.quit
```

Config startup
--------------

`./bin/evangeline conf` now accepts either an encrypted config or a plaintext
config. If `conf` is plaintext, Evangeline writes `conf.dec` as the backup,
saves the encrypted `conf`, and continues startup in the same command.

`./bin/evangeline -c conf` is still available as the legacy "encrypt and exit"
mode.

IPv6 botnet links
-----------------

Use `myipv6 ::` to make the botnet listener IPv6-only. `listen` is encrypted by
default. `hub` and `alt` accept IPv4, IPv6, and hostnames; botnet links use SSL
by default, so no `ssl:` prefix is required:

```
myipv6 ::
listen 9000
hub ::1 9000 botpassword
hub hub.example.net 9000 botpassword
alt ::1 9000
```

Leaf fallback uses the primary `hub` first, then learned slave endpoints, then
the hub as the last resort. When the primary slave is reachable again, the leaf
switches back automatically.

Modules
-------

`modules/otp-sec.cpp` provides authenticator-based IRC OP flows.

QR/TOTP OP:

```
.qrcode enable
.qrcode set <handle> enable
/msg BotNick qr-setup
/msg BotNick qr-op 123456
```

The module does not grant privileges by itself. After a valid code it calls the
shared OP path, so channel/global flags are checked by the same logic used by
the classic OP command.

Email OTP OP:

```
.email-otp enable
.email-otp set smtp-host smtp.example.com
.email-otp set smtp-port 587
.email-otp set smtp-security starttls
.email-otp user <handle> set-email user@example.com
.email-otp user <handle> enable
/msg BotNick email-otp
/msg BotNick op-email 123456
```

OTP values are single-use, expire, and are rate-limited. SMTP runs in a worker
thread and does not call IRC or userlist APIs directly.

`modules/fast-login.cpp` provides TOTP partyline login:

```
.fastlogin enable
.fastlogin <handle> enable
/msg BotNick owner-setup
```

After QR setup, the user can connect to the partyline and log in with one line:

```
<handle> <6-digit authenticator code>
```

FastLogin bypasses the normal ownerpass/login/password prompts only for handles
explicitly enabled by an owner with the required privileges. It enforces `+p`,
per-IP failure counters, temporary lockouts, and TOTP replay protection.

More details are in `QR-OTP.md`, `EMAIL-OTP.md`, and `FAST-LOGIN.md`.
